//! Digital signals.

import std::sources::Rail

/// A digital line together with the rail that defines its logic levels. The line swings
/// between the rail's `neg` and `pos`.
///
/// The four levels are voltages relative to the rail's `neg`, as a part's datasheet gives
/// them, and a part states those that it has. A connection between two lines does not
/// equate them: each belongs to its own pin (ADR 0046).
///
/// - `vih`: the lowest voltage an input reads as high.
/// - `vil`: the highest voltage an input reads as low.
/// - `voh`: the lowest voltage an output drives a high to, at the load the datasheet names.
/// - `vol`: the highest voltage an output drives a low to, at the load the datasheet names.
///
/// After the rails are solved, the solver requires of each output on a net that its `voh`
/// is always at least the `vih` of every input there, and its `vol` at most their `vil`; it
/// says "not evaluated" when a level has no value or the two grounds are not the same net.
/// The pin `.line` has `.abs_min` and `.abs_max`, which the solver checks against the
/// worst-case voltage of its net in the same way.
interface Logic {
    .line: Pin
    .reference: Rail
    .vih: Voltage
    .vil: Voltage
    .voh: Voltage
    .vol: Voltage
}
